{"name":"Tin Foil","hex":"#acb0b0","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tin foil","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of tinfoil."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tin_foil"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tin foil","blocks":[{"text":"Tin foil, also spelled tinfoil, is a thin foil made of tin. The term is also sometimes used to refer to aluminium foil, although this is avoided in technical contexts."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tin_foil"},{"title":"Foil (metal)","blocks":[{"text":"A foil is a very thin sheet of metal, typically made by hammering or rolling. Foils are most easily made with malleable metal, such as aluminium, copper, tin, and gold. Foils usually bend under their own weight and can be torn easily. For example, aluminium foil is usually about 1⁄1000 inch (0.025 mm), whereas gold can be made into foil only a few atoms thick, called gold leaf. Extremely thin foil is called metal leaf. Leaf tears very easily and must be picked up with special brushes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Aluminio.jpg/330px-Aluminio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foil_(metal)"},{"title":"Tin","blocks":[{"text":"Tin is a chemical element; it has the symbol Sn and atomic number 50. A metallic-gray metal, tin is soft enough to be cut with little force, and a bar of tin can be bent by hand with little effort. When bent, a bar of tin makes a sound, the so-called \"tin cry\", as a result of twinning in tin crystals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Sn-Alpha-Beta.jpg/330px-Sn-Alpha-Beta.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tin"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/DSC01266_-_A_Delicate_Balance_%2829773086657%29.jpg/500px-DSC01266_-_A_Delicate_Balance_%2829773086657%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/DSC01266_-_A_Delicate_Balance_%2829773086657%29.jpg/1280px-DSC01266_-_A_Delicate_Balance_%2829773086657%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/DSC01266_-_A_Delicate_Balance_%2829773086657%29.jpg/500px-DSC01266_-_A_Delicate_Balance_%2829773086657%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tin foil phonograph","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADSC01266_-_A_Delicate_Balance_(29773086657).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Tin-2.jpg/500px-Tin-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Tin-2.jpg/500px-Tin-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Solidified droplet of molten tin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATin-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Sword_bronze_age_%282nd_version%29.jpg/500px-Sword_bronze_age_%282nd_version%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Sword_bronze_age_%282nd_version%29.jpg/500px-Sword_bronze_age_%282nd_version%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ceremonial giant bronze dirk of the Plougrescant-Ommerschans type, Plougrescant, France, 1500–1300 BC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASword_bronze_age_(2nd_version).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Tin%28II%29-chloride-xtal-1996-3D-balls-front.png/500px-Tin%28II%29-chloride-xtal-1996-3D-balls-front.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thu